{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_8b32f96de5d193a56f4dbeaaa57572231b824a7375f320f378eef02e4163e729", "title": "2006 Kansas Jayhawks football team", "text": "The 2006 Kansas Jayhawks football team represented the University of Kansas during the 2006 NCAA Division I FBS football season. They participated as members of the Big 12 Conference in the North Division. The team played their home games at Memorial Stadium in Lawrence, Kansas. They were coached by Mark Mangino.", "score": 30.5112}, {"id": "hp_doc_9e8f93e267f082372a01077f6d73b8521b53e5d72631d7f250f158dd98aa6d9f", "title": "1900 Kansas Jayhawks football team", "text": "The 1900 Kansas Jayhawks football team represented the University of Kansas as an independent during the 1900 college football season. In August 1900, Lawrence W. Boynton, a recent graduate of Cornell, accepted an offer to serve as the Kansas football coach. In their only season under Boynton, the Jayhawks compiled a 2–5–2 record and were outscored by opponents by a combined total of 118 to 75. The Jayhawks played their home games at McCook Field in Lawrence, Kansas. Charles Wilcox was the team captain.", "score": 29.509}, {"id": "hp_doc_6c8d59b302389b3b9ea310d637e7b961ae56bae0880e4fb04215d3cae76d0200", "title": "1903 Kansas Jayhawks football team", "text": "The 1903 Kansas Jayhawks football team represented the University of Kansas as an independent during the 1903 college football season. In January 1903, Kansas hired Boss Weeks, who was the quarterback of Fielding H. Yost's 1901 and 1902 \"Point-a-Minute\" teams at Michigan, as its new head coach. In their only season under Weeks, the Jayhawks compiled a 6–3 record and outscored opponents by a combined total of 118 to 39. The Jayhawks played their home games at McCook Field in Lawrence, Kansas. Alpha Brumage was the team captain.", "score": 28.7198}], "remaining_searches": 2}
